2. Source code and self-built copies

You may read and audit the source code, learn from it, modify it, and compile
and use your own builds free of charge for your own use, including professional
and commercial work. You may install those builds on any number of devices you
use. An organization may build the Software for its own internal users and use
it internally, without distributing builds outside that organization.

You may share the source code, including modifications, and maintain public
source-only forks, provided you retain this license, copyright and third-party
notices, identify your modifications, and apply this license to the Nozhik-derived
code you distribute. You may submit patches and pull requests. These permissions
do not grant a right to use Nozhik code in another product under different terms.

3. Compiled builds and redistribution

Without separate written permission from the Licensor, you may not distribute,
sell, sublicense, publish, or otherwise provide compiled copies of the Software
to other users outside the internal-use permission in section 2. This includes
unmodified and modified builds, official and self-built copies, executables,
libraries, installers, application bundles and container images containing such
builds, whether provided free of charge or for payment. Renaming or rebranding
a build does not remove this restriction.

Copies between your own devices and backups for your own use are permitted.
Sharing source code as allowed in section 2 is permitted. These restrictions do
not restrict rights independently granted by third-party licenses or mandatory
applicable law.

4. Purchased official builds

A purchase of an official build grants one individual user a non-exclusive,
perpetual right to use that build for personal, professional or commercial work,
subject to activation and a limit of three active devices per user for that
licensed product. An active device is a device with an activation that has not
been deactivated or expired, whether or not the application is currently running.
The limit is not a limit of three concurrent application sessions. An organization
may purchase licenses for individual users. A user license may not be shared with
other individuals. Redistribution remains subject to section 3.

Activation uses a license key and an email address controlled by the licensed
user. The first activation binds an unassigned key to that email address.
Activation of an additional device requires a one-time verification code sent
to the email address already bound to the license. Changing that address requires
verification through the existing email address and confirmation of the new
address, or recovery approved by the Licensor after verification of ownership.
Possession of a license key alone does not authorize changing a bound address.

Users may deactivate their devices to transfer an activation to another device,
subject to the three-device limit. Activation and periodic renewal of device
authorization require an internet connection when the activation system is
available. A device authorization permits offline use for up to seven days from
its issuance or last successful renewal. Deactivated devices cannot renew their
authorization; an offline authorization already issued to such a device may
remain usable until it expires. A replacement activation may therefore briefly
overlap with an unexpired offline authorization on a deactivated device.

Expiry of a device authorization does not end the perpetual purchase right;
continued use requires renewed authorization. No subscription or recurring fee
is imposed by this license. Until the activation system is implemented and made
available for an official build, technical activation and renewal are not
required for that build; the personal-use and three-active-device restrictions
still apply. During that period, a device on which the user keeps an installed
copy for use counts as active; backup copies alone do not count as active devices.
This revision does not retroactively replace the terms supplied
with an earlier purchase unless the purchaser agrees to the replacement terms.

This permission also covers official updates supplied to that user as part of
the purchase. A purchase does not by itself promise future releases, lifetime
updates or support; any additional entitlement must be stated at purchase.

